From: Luissalet <luismasc16@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 02:24:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 22/36] Give every generated image a recipe, written into the
 PNG
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The loop the recipe needed to be worth anything: the adapter now builds
one per image and stamps it into the file before handing it back, so a
picture exported from the Gallery still knows how it was made when it
comes back a year later on another machine.

One recipe per image, not per job: sd.cpp seeds a batch as seed, seed+1,
… in index order, and a recipe naming the batch's first seed would
reproduce a different picture than the one it is attached to.

Our chunk goes in beside the runtime's A1111 `parameters` line, never
instead of it. That line is what Diffusion Toolkit, the WebUI's PNG-info
tab and Civitai read, and a file leaving Writers Hoard without it would
be illegible everywhere the writer took it.

sdModelAssets reads the digests from the catalogue rather than
re-hashing: downloadVerified refuses any byte that disagrees with the
pin, and refreshModels only reports a model installed when the receipt
carries those digests for every file, so the catalogue digest already IS
the digest of the file on disk. LoRAs have no catalogue entry — the
reader drops them in — so those are hashed for real, streamed.

A model that is not installed produces no recipe at all rather than one
that cannot name its own weights: a record that could not identify the
files it used would leave a reader unable to tell a faithful replay from
a substitution, which is the one thing recipes exist to prevent.

